          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Political Leadership</strong><br>\nDeng never held office as the head of state, party, or government, yet he was considered the \"architect\" of modern China. His leadership style was pragmatic and focused on achieving economic progress while maintaining a one-party state. Deng emphasized practical results over ideological purity, which was a departure from Maoist policies.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The One-Child Policy</strong><br>\nOne of the controversial policies during Deng's time was the introduction of the one-child policy in 1979. This policy was implemented to control the rapid population growth but led to unintended social issues. Despite its controversies, Deng believed that population control was crucial for economic development.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Foreign Relations and Openness</strong><br>\nDeng improved China's foreign relations, especially with the West, by opening up the economy to international investment and trade. He normalized relations with the United States and visited several countries to establish China as a significant global player, signaling a new era in Chinese diplomacy.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
